## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests understanding of stack augmentation, a practical pattern for maintaining auxiliary state. It demonstrates the ability to design data structures that support efficient queries while handling edge cases like duplicates and empty states.

## Key Concepts
- Two auxiliary stacks (minStack and maxStack) track running minima and maxima in sync with the main stack
- Each push/pop operation updates all three stacks atomically, preserving O(1) time complexity
- Duplicates are handled naturally since the min/max is recalculated at each step
- Empty stack edge cases must be handled (typically by checking stack length before accessing top)

## Code Example
```javascript
class MinMaxStack {
  constructor() {
    this.stack = [];
    this.minStack = [];
    this.maxStack = [];
  }

  push(x) {
    this.stack.push(x);
    this.minStack.push(
      this.minStack.length ? Math.min(x, this.minStack[this.minStack.length - 1]) : x
    );
    this.maxStack.push(
      this.maxStack.length ? Math.max(x, this.maxStack[this.maxStack.length - 1]) : x
    );
  }

  pop() {
    if (this.stack.length === 0) return undefined;
    this.minStack.pop();
    this.maxStack.pop();
    return this.stack.pop();
  }

  top() {
    return this.stack[this.stack.length - 1];
  }

  getMin() {
    return this.minStack[this.minStack.length - 1];
  }

  getMax() {
    return this.maxStack[this.maxStack.length - 1];
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Maintains compact histogram with dynamic quantile queries in O(log D).

## Key Concepts
- Fenwick tree for prefix counts over fixed domain
- Lower_bound on Fenwick to locate quantile index
- Edge handling when total is zero or p at bounds

## Code Example
```javascript
class HistogramQuantile {
  constructor(){
    this.N = 2001;
    this.ft = new Array(this.N+1).fill(0);
  }
  update(i, delta){
    for (let x = i; x <= this.N; x += x & -x) this.ft[x] += delta;
  }
  insertValue(v){ this.update(v + 1001, 1); }
  eraseValue(v){ this.update(v + 1001, -1); }
  sum(i){ let s = 0; for (let x = i; x > 0; x -= x & -x) s += this.ft[x]; return s; }
  lowerBound(k){
    let idx = 0, bit = 1 << 11; // > 2001
    let acc = 0;
    for (; bit; bit >>= 1){
      const next = idx + bit;
      if (next <= this.N && acc + this.ft[next] < k){ idx = next; acc += this.ft[next]; }
    }
    return idx + 1;
  }
  findQuantile(p){
    const total = this.sum(this.N);
    if (total === 0) return null;
    const k = Math.ceil(p * total);
    const i = this.lowerBound(k);
    return i - 1001;
  }
}
```

